// Probe: THE `pipeline values:` BLOCK FOR A KEYWORD PIPE STAGE.
//
// EVERY ASSERTION IN THIS FILE FAILS ON PURPOSE. The sweep compares the stdout
// of runs where every assertion PASSES, so a failure-report divergence is
// invisible to it by construction — the same reason testdata/pipe_stages.nomi
// exists. What is compared here is the REPORT TEXT.
//
// pipestage.go excluded `|> if` and `|> case` from the recordable shapes, on the
// recorded reason that each "consume the piped value through their own
// machinery, and three of the four are already refused as pipe stages in their
// own right". That reason rested on the refusal, and this slice lifted it — so
// the exclusion was re-DERIVED against the interpreter rather than re-checked.
//
// The answer: eval's `finish` calls env.recordPipelineStage for EVERY stage
// shape, so the stage list is a function of the spine here exactly as it is for
// a call chain. Both spellings are below because they produce different chains:
// the keyword form desugars to TWO pipe nodes and therefore THREE rows (the
// leftmost value, the condition prefix, the whole chain), and the bare form to
// ONE pipe node and therefore TWO. A fixture with only one spelling cannot see
// a lowering that recorded the wrong number of rows.
//
// The third case is the one that would be a WRONG report rather than an absent
// one: a chain mixing an ordinary call stage with a keyword stage, where the
// prefix texts must be the CUMULATIVE source of each prefix and not the stage's
// own.
//
// Note the rendered text of a keyword stage's last row is the KEYWORD spelling
// (`|> if Int.equal?(12) { False } else { True }`), not the desugared double
// pipe — the formatter renders the parser's two nodes back to the one the user
// wrote, and both sides go through the same formatter — the reference through
// format.RenderNode and the artifact through renderNode at build time.
//
// THE LAST TWO TESTS ARE A DIFFERENT CONTRACT and they are the pair that makes
// gen.pipedCase observable. evalCaseMatchWithValue records the SCRUTINEE as a
// `values:` row — but only `if n.Value != nil` (eval.go:13251), and a BARE pipe
// stage's node has a nil Value. So the interpreter shows the scrutinee for a
// directly written `case` and NOT for the piped spelling, and a builder that
// spliced Value in without exempting the node would record a row the
// interpreter does not print.
//
// The scrutinee is `wrap(3)` rather than `Some(3)` deliberately: rt and eval
// both drop a row whose operand text reads exactly like its value, so
// `Some(3) = Some(3)` is suppressed on BOTH paths and the two spellings would
// present identically. `wrap(3) = Some(3)` is the row that distinguishes them.
fn wrap(n: Int): Maybe<Int> {
    Some(n)
}

test "a keyword-prefixed if stage reports three pipeline rows" {
    flag =
        12
        |> if Int.equal?(12) { False } else { True }

    assert flag
}

test "a bare case stage reports two pipeline rows" {
    flag =
        Some(3)
        |> case {
                Some(_) -> False
                None -> True
            }

    assert flag
}

test "a call stage before a keyword stage reports cumulative prefixes" {
    flag =
        [1, 2, 3]
        |> Iter.count()
        |> if Int.equal?(3) { False } else { True }

    assert flag
}

test "a piped case scrutinee contributes no values row" {
    assert (wrap(3) |> case {
            Some(n) -> n
            None -> 0
        }) == 99
}

test "a directly written case scrutinee does contribute one" {
    assert (case wrap(3) {
        Some(n) -> n
        None -> 0
    }) == 99
}
